WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.929
April 10

2
00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:04.635
Dear Mr. Rich Man,

3
00:00:04.636 --> 00:00:08.177
Here's your check for fifty dollars.

4
00:00:08.178 --> 00:00:11.839
Thank you very much, but I can't keep it.

5
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.156
I'm sorry I wrote all that silly stuff about the hat shop.

6
00:00:16.157 --> 00:00:21.107
It was just that I'd never seen anything like it.

7
00:00:21.108 --> 00:00:25.551
I wasn't hinting that you should buy me hats!

8
00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:29.113
And I was definitely not begging!

9
00:00:29.114 --> 00:00:34.611
My allowance is generous enough that I can buy all the hats I need.

10
00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:38.838
I don't want to take any more money from you than I have to.

11
00:00:38.839 --> 00:00:40.364
Sincerely yours,

12
00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:44.103
Jerusha Abbott

13
00:00:44.104 --> 00:00:45.460
April 11

14
00:00:45.461 --> 00:00:48.249
Dearest Daddy Longlegs,

15
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.726
Will you please forgive me for yesterday's letter?

16
00:00:52.727 --> 00:00:58.809
After I mailed it, I went to the post office and tried to get it back.

17
00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:05.421
I waited in line forever.

18
00:01:05.422 --> 00:01:07.684
Finally it was my turn.

19
00:01:07.685 --> 00:01:12.185
"I mailed a letter and now I need it back," I said to the clerk.

20
00:01:12.186 --> 00:01:15.549
I tapped my fingers impatiently on the counter.

21
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.552
"I can't do that, miss," he said.

22
00:01:18.553 --> 00:01:21.132
"It's against the rules."

23
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:24.174
"But I only mailed it an hour ago!

24
00:01:24.175 --> 00:01:27.219
Surely it hasn't left the post office.

25
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.259
Can't you see if it's still here? Please!"

26
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.858
"It's against the rules," he repeated.

27
00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:39.579
"Once a letter has been mailed, the post office can't return it to the sender.

28
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.840
You'll have to write him again. Next!"

29
00:01:42.841 --> 00:01:46.265
The clerk motioned to the person behind me in line.

30
00:01:46.266 --> 00:01:50.619
"How do you know it was addressed to a man?" I asked.

31
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.245
"This is a college town full of young ladies.

32
00:01:54.246 --> 00:01:56.307
These things happen all the time."

33
00:01:56.308 --> 00:01:58.849
The clerk sneered at me.

34
00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:06.238
"A young lady writes something in anger to her young man, and then has second thoughts.

35
00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:09.024
Just write him another letter today.

36
00:02:09.025 --> 00:02:12.303
I'm sure your young man will understand."

37
00:02:12.304 --> 00:02:18.313
"But . . . but . . ." I was so angry I could hardly speak.

38
00:02:18.314 --> 00:02:22.046
I turned to go, but then I thought of something to say.

39
00:02:22.047 --> 00:02:24.508
"He's not a young man!"

40
00:02:24.509 --> 00:02:31.363
Both the clerk and the lady behind me looked shocked as I stormed out of the post office.

41
00:02:31.364 --> 00:02:36.130
Now it's the middle of the night.

42
00:02:36.131 --> 00:02:41.202
I'm sitting in bed, writing on paper torn from my history notebook.

43
00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:46.601
I've been awake for hours thinking about what a horrible person I am.

44
00:02:46.602 --> 00:02:51.482
I'm truly sorry that I returned your check with such a rude letter.

45
00:02:51.483 --> 00:02:55.631
You're very kind to take so much trouble over a silly hat.

46
00:02:55.632 --> 00:02:59.729
But I really can't accept your check. You

47
00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.889
see, I'm different from other girls. They

48
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.099
can accept money from other people. They

49
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.408
have aunts and uncles and grandparents. But

50
00:03:11.409 --> 00:03:13.682
I have no one. Yes,

51
00:03:13.683 --> 00:03:21.379
I like to pretend you're part of my family, but really I'm alone in this world. Sometimes

52
00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:28.034
that idea fills me with so much fear that I can hardly breathe. So

53
00:03:28.035 --> 00:03:31.426
I try not to think about it. I

54
00:03:31.427 --> 00:03:38.010
can't accept any more money than necessary because I want to pay you back. Even

55
00:03:38.011 --> 00:03:45.438
if I become a famous author, I won't be able to repay a huge debt. I

56
00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:52.760
love pretty hats and things, but I must not go into debt for them. Do

57
00:03:52.761 --> 00:03:54.556
you understand? Please

58
00:03:54.557 --> 00:03:58.288
forgive me for being so rude. I

59
00:03:58.289 --> 00:04:02.518
have an awful habit of writing letters when I'm upset. I

60
00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:06.500
should stop and think before I mail them. If

61
00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:11.394
I sometimes seem thoughtless and ungrateful, I never mean it. In

62
00:04:11.395 --> 00:04:17.455
my heart I thank you for the life and independence you've given me. My

63
00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:24.925
childhood was just one long stretch of boredom, and now I'm so happy every moment. I

64
00:04:24.926 --> 00:04:27.068
can't believe it's true. I

65
00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:30.690
feel like a heroine in a novel. Speaking

66
00:04:30.691 --> 00:04:34.518
of novels . . . I've been reading Jane Eyre .

67
00:04:34.519 --> 00:04:37.377
Did you know that Jane was an orphan?

68
00:04:37.378 --> 00:04:41.212
The other day I was reading about her troubles at the orphanage.

69
00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:45.331
I got so angry that I had to take a walk!

70
00:04:45.332 --> 00:04:49.482
I understood exactly how she felt.

71
00:04:49.483 --> 00:04:55.321
I'm not saying that the John Grier Home was like poor Jane's orphanage.

72
00:04:55.322 --> 00:04:59.537
We had plenty to eat and warm clothes to wear.

73
00:04:59.538 --> 00:05:05.254
But there was one similarity: Our lives were boring and uneventful.

74
00:05:05.255 --> 00:05:10.407
Nothing nice ever happened, except we got ice cream on Sundays.

75
00:05:10.408 --> 00:05:14.890
And even that was predictable because it happened every Sunday.

76
00:05:14.891 --> 00:05:17.864
We never had any adventures.

77
00:05:17.865 --> 00:05:23.016
Everybody likes a few surprises in life; that's a normal thing to want.

78
00:05:23.017 --> 00:05:28.735
Before I fall asleep at night, I like to imagine the perfect orphanage.

79
00:05:28.736 --> 00:05:36.293
I plan out the smallest details: the meals, the clothes, the lessons, the entertainment.

80
00:05:36.294 --> 00:05:42.312
I even imagine the punishments because I'm sure some of my orphans will be naughty.

81
00:05:42.313 --> 00:05:46.813
But I'll make sure all the orphans are happy.

82
00:05:46.814 --> 00:05:51.590
Everybody should have a happy childhood to look back on.

83
00:05:51.591 --> 00:05:57.698
I didn't have a happy childhood, but I'm very happy now, thanks to you.

84
00:05:57.699 --> 00:05:58.933
Love always,

85
00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:01.037
Judy

